  "body": "Asked for by the maintainer (2026-11-13, in chat): \"Fixtures should be able to also generate Lathe chuck, both 3, 4 yaw, and collet type.\" (read as 3-jaw, 4-jaw and collet chucks).\n\nWhat is there today: the built-in fixture library generates milling workholding from parameters - vises (#788, #787, #820), grid/dowel plates (#819) and accessories (#789). A lathe setup has no generated workholding body: a chuck is only there if the user imports one (STEP/DXF, #744 is the open import work), and the workholding max rpm (#1031) and the holder-against-jaws check (#1037) have nothing built in to work on.\n\nWanted: in the fixture library, three parametric lathe workholding generators, made the same way the built-in vises are (original geometry from parameters, no vendor model):\n\n- 3-jaw chuck (self-centring): body diameter and length, jaw length / height / step, through bore, grip diameter set from the stock (outside or inside grip).\n- 4-jaw chuck: same parameters with four jaws; independent jaws are allowed an offset each, or the first version is self-centring only and says so.\n- Collet chuck: nose diameter and length, collet bore set from the stock diameter, nose stick-out.\n\nEach one:\n- is created by an undoable command with its MCP tool (AGENTS.md \"Commands ship with an MCP tool\"), and placed on the spindle axis of a turning setup with \"Fit to the stock\";\n- is a fixture body like the vises: shown in the viewport and simulation, and judged by the existing turning collision checks (holder and insert against the jaws, #1037) - a generated chuck must never be skipped by a check that an imported one gets;\n- carries its max rpm into the setup's workholding limit (#1031) when the user enters one;\n- is refused by name in a milling setup unless it sits on a rotary axis (a 4th-axis chuck is #821's neighbourhood; out of scope here unless it falls out for free).\n\nRelated: #744 (fixtures as spun bodies, import), #339 (umbrella: handle fixtures/vises), #832, #821.\n\nDone means: the Fixtures page offers \"3-jaw chuck\", \"4-jaw chuck\" and \"Collet chuck\" for a turning setup; each is generated from its parameters, fits to the stock, undoes and redoes, has an MCP tool, and a turning pass that would run the holder into a generated jaw is refused exactly like with an imported chuck (pinned by tests, one per chuck type for generation and one for the collision refusal).\n",
